 ## Overview
-Safe Raymarching Step Size is a method for determining the step length $\Delta t$ along a ray in volume rendering and implicit surface rendering to prevent the ray from overshooting geometry or density features [S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of artifacts caused by large steps that skip over thin structures or surface intersections, particularly when the distance field is distorted or when sampling neural implicit representations [S1, S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by explicitly combining clamping of the distance value, a configurable ratio multiplier, and strict boundary checks to ensure numerical stability and correctness in both parallel and recursive marching implementations [S2, S3, unofficial].
+Safe Raymarching Step Size is a method for determining the distance to advance along a ray in Signed Distance Field (SDF) rendering to guarantee that the ray never overshoots or penetrates the implicit surface [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of geometric artifacts and missed intersections caused by inaccurate distance estimates, particularly when SDFs are distorted by boolean operations or domain repetition [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard sphere tracing which assumes the SDF is a perfect lower bound, this method introduces safety margins (clamping, ratios) or grid-aware bounds to ensure numerical stability [S2, S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Adaptive Step Size Control](9843-adaptive-step-size-control.md) by prioritizing strict non-penetration guarantees over aggressive convergence speed, often at the cost of increased sample counts [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method determines the step size $\Delta t_i$ based on the current position $\mathbf{p}_i$, the ray direction $\mathbf{d}$, and the local distance field value $f(\mathbf{p}_i)$ [S2, S3, unofficial]. It operates on tensor-based batches of rays for parallel execution and recursive structures for adaptive execution [S2, unofficial].
+The method operates by computing a step size $\Delta d$ that is guaranteed to be less than or equal to the true distance to the surface. Two primary implementations exist in the source literature: a neural-decoder-based approach using clamping and ratios [S2, unofficial], and a grid-based approach using AABB dimensions [S3, unofficial].
 
